window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][14] = "<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) &quot;First-time adopters in their first two weeks. I chose them by pain and by fit with our services, and I gave up families who already own pets.&quot; (b) &quot;It happens to nearly every adopter, costs them a vet visit or a night of worry, and they already work around it by searching forums. I'd confirm that with interviews.&quot; (c) &quot;A different kind of solution: instead of a guide, I could connect them with a mentor who has just adopted. It is slower to scale and may help more.&quot; (d) &quot;Misuse and safety: unverified advice about pet health could do harm, so I'd restrict medical guidance to vet-reviewed content and route emergencies to a vet.&quot; (e) &quot;Sure. The problem I'm solving is the first-two-weeks uncertainty, and my first idea is a daily checklist with vet-reviewed answers.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Record yourself replying, and check that each reply is under 20 seconds and returns to the spine.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Short replies keep the answer moving and can skip nuance; offer more if the interviewer asks.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for four weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: user and problems.</em> Take a prompt from the exercises or the questions (IQ-C07-01, IQ-C07-02, EX-C07-04) and spend ten minutes on the goal, the user, and a ranked pain-point table. Say the criterion aloud.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: range and recommendation.</em> Use IQ-C07-03, IQ-C07-04 or EX-C07-03. Produce three solutions that differ in kind, fill the grid, and say the recommendation line with a stated cost. Twelve minutes.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: surfaces and risks.</em> Use IQ-C07-05, IQ-C07-06 or EX-C07-05 and EX-C07-06. Describe moments for a watch, a speaker or a physical device, then run the responsible-design check and state defaults and a launch condition. Fifteen minutes.</li>\n<li><em>Week 4: full rounds.</em> Run two 45-minute answers and one 25-minute answer (EX-C07-09) on prompts you have not seen, with a partner who uses the interventions in section 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to interrupt with &quot;Who is the user?&quot; and &quot;What could go wrong?&quot; at random points, and to challenge your chosen user once with &quot;Why not [other segment]?&quot; (as in IQ-C07-07). Practice answering in under a minute and returning to the spine.</p>\n<p><strong>Record and review.</strong> Record every full run. Listen once for structure, once for wording. Count how many times you described a screen when you could have described a moment, and how many solutions were variants of one idea.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I clarify and state a goal before designing?</li>\n<li>Did I choose a specific user by a stated criterion, and say what I gave up?</li>\n<li>Did I rank the pain points and choose with a reason?</li>\n<li>Were my solutions different in kind, each linked to the problem?</li>\n<li>Did I name the deciding criterion and recommend one option with its cost?</li>\n<li>Did I describe the first version as moments, and say what comes next?</li>\n<li>Did the primary metric match the chosen problem, with a guardrail and a counter metric?</li>\n<li>Did I name two or three harms with a design response and a default?</li>\n<li>Did I say what would change my mind?</li>\n<li>Did I finish with the two-sentence close from section 4?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the user, the chosen problem, the recommendation, your score out of ten and the step you rushed. After eight drills, look for the step you skip most often (usually the risks or the metrics) and drill that step alone for a week.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Choose a feature your team shipped or is building. Write the user (a segment with a criterion), the pain point it addresses (observable, with the workaround), and the goal ladder (FW-C07-02). Ask a teammate whether they would have written the same problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Write a one-page design brief for an upcoming piece of work: the user, the ranked pain points, three solutions that differ in kind, the evaluation grid, the recommendation with its stated cost, and the metric with a guardrail and a counter metric. Share it with your designer and an engineer before a solution is chosen, and record what changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Run the responsible-design check (FW-C07-06) on one feature in development. List the two or three most serious risks, the defaults, and the launch condition. Bring them to the review, and note which became requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Watch three people use your product for a task you care about, without helping. Write down each moment of hesitation and each workaround. Compare them with the pain points you had ranked.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Take a decision your team made recently and write the choice as an option grid, with the options that were not chosen. Ask the team whether the grid describes the discussion, and what was missing.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the design brief (a time you framed a problem and chose a solution with a stated cost), the responsible-design check (a time you spotted a risk early and changed a design), and the observation notes (a time you learned from watching users and changed a priority). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c07-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c07-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>A design answer is a chain: a goal, a specific user chosen by a stated criterion, a ranked pain point, solutions that differ in kind, one recommendation with its cost, and a way to measure it.</li>\n<li>Write pain points as something you could watch happen, and rank them by frequency, severity and what people do about them today.</li>\n<li>Describe moments, not screens, and fit the design to the surface: what the device senses, what it can compute locally, how much attention it has, and what cannot change after shipping.</li>\n<li>Run the responsible-design check on every answer: name two or three harms for this product, a concrete design response and a protective default, and say when you would not launch.</li>\n<li>Finish with a decision and a way to be wrong: the recommendation, its sacrifice, the metric with a counter metric, and what would change your mind.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c07-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Don Norman, <em>The Design of Everyday Things</em> (revised and expanded edition, 2013). A foundation for thinking about how people interact with products, including the idea that errors are often design failures.</li>\n<li>Kat Holmes, <em>Mismatch: How Inclusion Shapes Design</em> (2018). Inclusive design, and the idea that mismatches between people and products create exclusion. Related Microsoft materials on inclusive design are available from the company's design site <code>[V: MIT Press, 16 October 2018, ISBN 9780262038881]</code>.</li>\n<li>Apple, <em>Human Interface Guidelines</em> (online, updated regularly). Public design guidance for Apple's platforms, useful for the vocabulary of devices and context <code>[V: Apple Human Interface Guidelines]</code>.</li>\n<li>Marty Cagan, <em>Inspired</em> (2nd edition, 2017). Product discovery and the role of the product manager in choosing the problem.</li>\n<li>Jake Knapp with John Zeratsky and Braden Kowitz, <em>Sprint</em> (2016). A five-day method for defining a problem, sketching solutions and testing with users.</li>\n<li>Gayle Laakmann McDowell and Jackie Bavaro, <em>Cracking the PM Interview</em> (2013). A widely used guide with chapters on product design questions <code>[V: CareerCup, first edition, 2 December 2013, ISBN 9780984782819]</code>.</li>\n<li>Apple, &quot;Apple Presents iPod,&quot; press release (23 October 2001) and &quot;Apple Unveils New iPods&quot; (17 July 2002), on Apple's newsroom archive; Apple's Form 10-K for fiscal 2003 and 2004 (SEC) for unit sales. The primary sources for CS-C07-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c08\"></span>\n<h2>C08 — Improving an Existing Product</h2>\n<span id=\"c08-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;How would you improve X?&quot; is one of the most common product questions, and it is a different question from &quot;design a product.&quot; The product already exists. It has users who rely on it, a business behind it, numbers that move, and a history that explains why it looks the way it does. The interviewer is watching whether you treat it as something that exists or as a blank page.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: improvement prompts about a well-known product (a maps app, a messaging app, a notes app, a video service) appear in product-sense rounds at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple and many others, often as the second or third question type after a pure design prompt <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. On the job it is the work most associate and early-career PMs do most of the time: a team owns an area of a live product and has to decide what to change.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> The same five things as a design round (structure, user choice, problem choice, solution quality, a decision with a measure), plus three that belong to this question <code>Inferred</code>: whether you understood what the product is for before changing it, whether you chose where to improve with a reason and some sense of size, and whether you thought about the people the change might hurt.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You state what the product is for, for users and for the company, and what you would look at to understand its current state.</li>\n<li>You choose an area to improve (a goal, a segment, a step in the journey) by a stated criterion, and say how much room there is.</li>\n<li>You name a specific problem at that step, with the evidence you have or would collect.</li>\n<li>You consider more than one kind of move (fix, add, remove, reposition) and choose with a rough size of the gain.</li>\n<li>You name who loses from the change and what you do about it.</li>\n<li>You define success with a guardrail and a counter metric, and say what would make you reverse.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end improvement answers: a wish list for an app the candidate likes, ignoring the existing users as if the product were new, not knowing what the product is for, reaching for a new feature when a fix or a removal would do, no sense of how big the gain could be, and no mention of who is worse off.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c08-c08-1-how-improving-differs-from-designing-from-scratch\"></span>\n<h4>C08.1 How improving differs from designing from scratch</h4>\n<p>C07 gave a spine for designing for a user. Improving an existing product keeps most of it and changes six things.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>In a design round</th>\n<th>In an improvement round</th>\n<th>Consequence for your answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>No users yet; you pick one</td>\n<td>Many users, already using it</td>\n<td>Choose which existing users and what they do today</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The goal is yours to set</td>\n<td>The product has a role and a goal</td>\n<td>Infer the goal first; do not invent one that ignores it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No numbers</td>\n<td>A funnel, metrics, a history</td>\n<td>Say what you would look at, and reason with plausible numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Any solution is open</td>\n<td>Constraints: habits, data, integrations, other devices</td>\n<td>Name the constraint that matters before proposing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A new thing cannot hurt anyone yet</td>\n<td>A change can hurt people who rely on the old way</td>\n<td>Say who loses and how you protect them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The solution is mostly an addition</td>\n<td>Fix, remove and reposition are live options</td>\n<td>Offer different kinds of move, not only new features</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The key shift is that you are looking for the most valuable change to something that works for many people, and the downside is real. A new feature that helps one segment but breaks a habit for a larger one is a worse product, however clever.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use the improvement spine.</strong> If the prompt asks for something new (a product that does not exist), use C07. If the prompt is about entering a market where the product does not yet operate, the questions change again (C09). If a metric has already moved and the question is why, you are diagnosing, not improving (C13). If the interviewer asks for a critique rather than an improvement, the method is in C11.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked &quot;How would you improve a maps app?&quot;, a candidate says at once, &quot;I'd add augmented-reality walking directions, because it's cool and nobody does it well.&quot; The interviewer asks who uses the product and what they do most. The candidate has no answer: they never said what the product is for, which users matter, or where it falls short. The idea might be sound, but there is nothing to show it is the best use of the team's time. A candidate who first says &quot;this is mostly used for driving directions and finding places, so I'd look at which journey step loses the most people&quot; has not yet proposed anything and is already ahead.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. First, claiming knowledge you do not have: &quot;I know their retention is low&quot; is a claim about a company's internal data. Say &quot;I'd expect&quot; and &quot;I'd check,&quot; and use public information, labeled as such. Second, anchoring on what you personally dislike. Your own pain is one data point; it is a hypothesis about a segment, not evidence.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-c08-2-reading-a-product-s-current-state\"></span>\n<h4>C08.2 Reading a product's current state</h4>\n<p>Before you choose what to improve, form a view of the product as it is. FW-C08-02 gives five questions: what role the product plays, who uses it and how, where the funnel leaks, which metrics are healthy and which are not, and what cannot change. In an interview you will not have the data, so you reason from public information and your own use, say so, and name what you would verify.</p>\n<p><strong>Role in the company.</strong> A product can be a revenue engine (it earns directly), a growth engine (it brings in users for other products), a retention anchor (it keeps users within a larger offering), a strategic bet (it exists to hold a position), or a utility (it must work and is rarely the reason someone chooses the company). The role changes what an improvement means. For a utility, a faster and more reliable core task is an improvement even if engagement falls. For a growth engine, a change that lifts sign-ups and lowers quality may still be wrong. State your assumption about the role in one sentence, and ask the interviewer to correct you.</p>\n<p><strong>Users.</strong> Name two or three <a href=\"#gl-user-segment\">user segment</a> groups by what they do, and which of them the product serves best today. Core users (most frequent, most dependent), casual users (occasional, lightly attached), and new users (still deciding) usually need different things, and an improvement for one often costs another.</p>\n<p><strong>Funnel.</strong> Walk the user's journey as a <a href=\"#gl-funnel\">funnel</a> (C05): how people arrive, start, complete the core task, return. Mark the steps where you would expect the largest loss and say why. A step with high traffic and low completion is a candidate; a step few people reach is not, however painful.</p>\n<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Name the <a href=\"#gl-north-star-metric\">north star metric</a> you believe the product follows, and one or two <a href=\"#gl-input-metric\">input metric</a> values. Say which look healthy and which do not, as hypotheses. If the interviewer gives numbers, use them and stop speculating.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> What cannot change easily: a large base of habitual users, integrations that others depend on, data stored in a certain format, a regulatory requirement, a platform rule, or hardware already in people's hands (C08.4).</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The five questions turn &quot;improve X&quot; into &quot;improve X for this role, these users, this step,&quot; which is the form of a decision. The interviewer sees that you start from the product, not from your own idea.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to do it at length.</strong> If the interviewer gives the goal (&quot;we want more people to finish setup&quot;), take it, spend a minute on users and funnel, and move on. Do not rebuild a state the prompt already supplies.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-c08-3-choosing-the-improvement-area-goal-segment-journey-ste\"></span>\n<h4>C08.3 Choosing the improvement area: goal, segment, journey step</h4>\n<p>An open improvement prompt has three choices inside it: the goal, the segment and the step.</p>\n<p><strong>Goal.</strong> Choose one that fits the product's role: more of the right people complete the core task, return more often, pay, or recommend. State it as an outcome for users, then the business measure (C07's goal ladder, FW-C07-02). If the interviewer has already named a goal, use it.</p>\n<p><strong>Segment.</strong> Use C06 and C07: by need and behavior, with a criterion. In an existing product you can also segment by relationship to it: new, casual, core, lapsed.</p>\n<p><strong>Step.</strong> Pick the point in the journey where improvement is most worth having. Three tests, which FW-C08-03 turns into a table:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Reach.</strong> How many of the chosen segment arrive at this step?</li>\n<li><strong>Pain or loss.</strong> How badly does the step go for them: drop-off, repeated effort, workarounds, complaints?</li>\n<li><strong>Headroom.</strong> <strong>Headroom</strong> is the gap between how a segment performs at a journey step today and a realistic level it could reach, which bounds how much an improvement there can add. A step where 95% of people already succeed has little headroom, however annoying it is to the other 5%. A step where 40% succeed and similar products reach 70% has a lot.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A fourth test, fit with the goal, decides ties. The step with the most headroom is not always the one that moves the goal: improving a step that few people reach, or that does not lead to the outcome you chose, wastes the gain.</p>\n<p><strong>Hidden risk.</strong> Choosing by pain you can imagine, not pain you can size. A vivid frustration affecting 2% of users loses to a mild one affecting half of them, unless the 2% matter for a stated reason (a segment that pays, or a safety issue).</p>\n<span id=\"c08-c08-4-constraints-of-an-existing-product-legacy-users-cannib\"></span>\n<h4>C08.4 Constraints of an existing product: legacy users, cannibalization, ecosystem effects</h4>\n<p>Three constraints deserve names, because naming them is half of handling them. FW-C08-04 is the scan.</p>\n<p><strong>Legacy users.</strong> A <strong>legacy user</strong> is a user (or customer) who depends on current behavior of the product (habits, integrations, stored data or contracts) so that a change creates cost for them even when it benefits others. Heavy users are often legacy users: they learned every shortcut. Enterprise customers are legacy users through their processes, training and integrations. The question to ask of any change is: who depends on how it works now, and what does the change cost them? Protect them with a path, not a promise: let them keep the old way for a period, migrate their data automatically, announce early, and measure their experience separately (FW-C08-07).</p>\n<p><strong>Cannibalization.</strong> <strong>Cannibalization</strong> is when a new feature or offer takes away use or revenue from an existing one of the same company, so that gross gains overstate net gains. A cheaper plan that mostly attracts existing full-price customers, or a new feed that mostly replaces time spent in the old one, adds little at the company level. Look for it by asking: where will this usage come from, new demand or an existing product? When the new thing is better for users, some cannibalization is the right outcome; the mistake is counting the moved use as growth.</p>\n<p><strong>Ecosystem effects.</strong> A change in one place has consequences elsewhere. <em>Across devices:</em> a feature on one device that others do not support creates inconsistent behavior, as when a note edited on one device cannot be opened on another. <em>With other systems:</em> integrations, partners and developers who built on current behavior (C19 treats platforms in full). <em>Between users:</em> in a product where people interact, a change for senders affects receivers, a <a href=\"#gl-network-effect\">network effect</a> working in reverse; a change that makes it easier to post can make the feed noisier for everyone who reads. <em>With rules and expectations:</em> platform policies, regulations and promises made to customers.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> In a mobile-first market, a large share of users are on mid-range or low-end Android phones, with limited storage and variable data connectivity <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: author's synthesis; no source checked]</code>. For an improvement, that turns into concrete constraints: app size, behavior on slow or intermittent connections, data use, and support for several languages. An improvement that adds weight to the app or requires a fast connection can be a regression for these users, so the legacy-user question has a regional form: who is on a device or connection that this change would break? Real-time account-to-account payments are widely used in India, notably through the Unified Payments Interface run by the National Payments Corporation of India <code>Public</code> <code>[V: NPCI, UPI product overview]</code>, so a payment step designed around cards alone may miss how many users prefer to pay. This box adds a regional constraint; it does not replace the global method.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The constraints are where an improvement answer shows maturity. Anyone can propose a feature; a candidate who says who would be hurt and how they would be protected has done the work of the job.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to dwell.</strong> Name the one or two constraints that matter for the chosen change; do not recite all three.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-c08-5-solution-types-fix-add-remove-reposition\"></span>\n<h4>C08.5 Solution types: fix, add, remove, reposition</h4>\n<p>C07 listed kinds of solution (eliminate, automate, assist, reframe, connect). For an existing product, a second cut matters: what you do to the product. FW-C08-05 names four moves.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Move</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n<th>Main risk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fix</td>\n<td>Repair something that is broken or slow</td>\n<td>A step with a high failure rate caused by a defect or poor wording</td>\n<td>Treated as boring; the gain is real and often the largest per unit of effort</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add</td>\n<td>A new capability or option</td>\n<td>A job users do elsewhere because the product cannot</td>\n<td>Complexity; cannibalization; more to maintain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remove</td>\n<td>Delete or simplify</td>\n<td>A feature that few use and many trip over; clutter</td>\n<td>Someone depended on it (legacy users); you may not know why it was added</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reposition</td>\n<td>Change what the product is for, who it is for, or how it is presented</td>\n<td>The product is used differently from how it is described</td>\n<td>Confuses current users; takes longer to show results</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Most candidates reach for Add. Interviewers notice when you offer a Fix or a Remove, because those show you understand the cost of complexity. Before you recommend a Remove, check why the thing exists (the &quot;fence&quot; test in C08, mental models, section 9): the person who added it may have solved a problem you cannot see.</p>\n<p>You can combine the two cuts. A &quot;fix&quot; can be an assist (better wording at a failing step) or an automation (filling in a field the user always enters). Use the move to describe what changes in the product and the kind to describe how it helps the user.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The four moves widen the range beyond new features, and they show that you think about the whole product as something to be kept simple.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to use all four.</strong> If time is short, show two with a contrast (for example, a fix and an add) and say why you chose one.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-c08-6-reasoning-about-metric-impact-and-sizing-the-opportuni\"></span>\n<h4>C08.6 Reasoning about metric impact and sizing the opportunity</h4>\n<p>An interviewer often asks &quot;how big could this be?&quot; You do not need accuracy; you need a defensible chain and an honest range (C04). FW-C08-06 gives the ladder:</p>\n<p><strong>Opportunity = users who reach the step × baseline rate at the step × expected lift × value of one success</strong>, then haircuts.</p>\n<p>Walk through an invented example. A recipe app has 2,000,000 weekly active users (invented). At a step called &quot;save your first recipe,&quot; 30% of users arrive: 600,000. Baseline completion is 40%: 240,000 complete it. A fix is expected to raise completion to 46%: 276,000, which is 36,000 more completions per week. Haircut 30% for novelty wearing off and overlap with users who would have completed anyway: 25,200. Value of a completion: in the data, people who complete the step retain 20 percentage points better than those who do not (invented), but that is a correlation: people who save a recipe are probably more interested to begin with. Credit half of it as causal: 25,200 × 0.20 × 0.5 = 2,520 additional retained weekly users, about 0.13% of the base (invented).</p>\n<p>Three lessons. The number is small, and that is useful: it says whether this is worth a team's quarter. Each assumption is visible, so the interviewer can challenge one and you can change it. And the biggest haircut belongs to the step that is easiest to inflate: treating a correlation between completing a step and retention as the effect of the step is a common error (<a href=\"#gl-proxy-metric\">proxy metric</a>, C05; and C14 on how to tell cause from correlation).</p>\n<p><strong>Check the result two ways.</strong> A second route: compare to what the metric did the last time something similar shipped, or to the gap between this step and a similar step in the product (the headroom). If the two disagree by more than a factor of two or three, one assumption is wrong (<a href=\"#gl-sanity-check\">sanity check</a>, C04).</p>\n<p><strong>Say the range.</strong> &quot;Between about 1,500 and 4,000 weekly retained users, which is under half a percent. The expected gain is small, so I'd want this to be cheap, and I'd look for a larger lever first.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A sized answer shows that you can choose between ideas, not only generate them, and that you know that most improvements are modest.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to size at length.</strong> Give the chain in two or three sentences unless the interviewer asks for more; the structure matters more than the arithmetic.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-c08-7-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h4>C08.7 Answer architecture</h4>\n<p>Section 4 sets out the improvement spine with a time budget: clarify the product and goal, read the current state, choose the area, find the problem, generate moves, size and choose, measure, check risks. It reuses the pieces from C07 and adds the state read, the constraint scan and the sizing.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-c08-8-worked-examples-consumer-enterprise-marketplace\"></span>\n<h4>C08.8 Worked examples: consumer, enterprise, marketplace</h4>\n<p>Section 5 gives four worked improvements: a consumer messaging setting, an enterprise reporting tool, a marketplace, and a photo-library product. Section 11 adds seven questions with model answers, including two on devices and services made by Apple and one each set on a Google and a Meta product.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Seven frameworks, set out below. The goal ladder (FW-C07-02), the pain-point matrix (FW-C07-03), the option evaluation grid (FW-C07-05), the responsible-design check (FW-C07-06), the segment scorecard (FW-C06-02) and the success-metric template (FW-C05-06) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c08-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C08-01 · The improvement answer spine · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> an improvement prompt about an existing product and 35 to 45 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> an answer with eight visible steps: clarify, state, area, problem, moves, size, measure, risks.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the interviewer will answer clarifying questions and may supply a goal or data.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> structure makes the reasoning scorable and can sound recited; the budget is a guide, not a quota.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> equal time on every step; no decision; treating it as a greenfield design; skipping the state read.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the messaging-settings improvement in Worked example 1, and the model answers in section 11.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Clarify → State → Area → Problem → Moves → Size and choose → Measure → Risks.</strong> The time budget is in section 4.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c08-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C08-02 · The product state card · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a named product and what you know of it from use and public information.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a five-line card: role, users, funnel, metrics, constraints, each with the assumption you are making and what you would check.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can infer a product's role from how it is offered and used; the interviewer will correct you.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a card fixes your assumptions early and can lock you into a wrong one; say so and invite correction.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> presenting guesses as facts; claiming internal knowledge; building a card so long it eats the time.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the state card for a photo-library product in Worked example 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Line</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Example answer (assumption)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Role</td>\n<td>What is this product for, for the company and for users?</td>\n<td>A utility that keeps users attached to the company's wider services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Users</td>\n<td>Who uses it, and which group is served best today?</td>\n<td>Heavy collectors served best; occasional users less so</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Funnel</td>\n<td>Where do people start, finish and return?</td>\n<td>Strong capture, weak retrieval</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics</td>\n<td>What does it follow, and what looks weak?</td>\n<td>Retrieval success and return visits look weaker than storage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constraints</td>\n<td>What cannot change easily?</td>\n<td>Years of stored data; a large base of habitual users</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c08-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C08-03 · The improvement-area selector · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a goal, two to four segments and the journey steps for each.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a table of candidate areas (segment and step) rated on reach, pain or loss, headroom and fit, with one or two chosen and a stated reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can estimate reach and headroom roughly from the funnel and comparisons.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> the table forces a choice and can imply false precision.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> choosing by the step you find annoying; ignoring headroom; rating everything high.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the selector for the marketplace in Worked example 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment and step</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Pain or loss</th>\n<th>Headroom</th>\n<th>Fit with goal</th>\n<th>Choice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Rule of thumb: choose the row where reach and headroom are both at least medium and fit is high. Name the row you rejected and why.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c08-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C08-04 · The constraint scan · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a proposed change.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> answers to four questions with a design response for each that applies: legacy users, cannibalization, ecosystem effects, and reversibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can reason about who depends on the current behavior and how it connects to other parts.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> thoroughness slows the answer; a short scan prevents a costly mistake.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a list of constraints with no response; treating all constraints as equal; forgetting the quiet ones (integrations, other devices).</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the scan for the enterprise reporting change in Worked example 2, and the sunset question in IQ-C08-06.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Typical response</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Legacy users</td>\n<td>Who depends on how this works today?</td>\n<td>Keep the old way for a period; automatic migration; early notice; measure them separately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cannibalization</td>\n<td>Will the usage or revenue come from something we already have?</td>\n<td>Count net, not gross; compare with a holdout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ecosystem</td>\n<td>What else does this touch: other devices, integrations, other users, rules?</td>\n<td>Test across the set; version interfaces; staged rollout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reversibility</td>\n<td>Can we undo it, and how fast?</td>\n<td>Release gradually; keep a rollback path (C17); treat irreversible steps with more evidence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c08-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C08-05 · The four moves · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a chosen problem at a chosen step.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> at least two moves of different types (fix, add, remove, reposition), each with how it works, why it helps, what it costs and what it risks, and one recommendation.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the solution space includes changing or deleting what exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> range versus depth; a fix is cheap and modest, a reposition is large and slow.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> only adding; recommending a removal without checking why the thing exists; a reposition with no evidence that users see the product differently.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the fix versus add choice in Worked example 3, and the remove decision in IQ-C08-06.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Test before recommending a Remove: who uses it, why it was built, what happens to them, and how you would know if the removal was a mistake.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c08-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C08-06 · The impact sizing ladder · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the chosen step, the product's scale and a proposed move.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a chain from users to value, with each assumption written, a haircut for novelty, overlap and correlation, a range, and a second-route check.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> order-of-magnitude values are enough to choose between ideas (C04).</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> quick sizing guides priority and can be wrong by a wide margin; the value is in the visible assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> treating a correlation as the effect; no haircut; a single point estimate; sizing the step when the goal is elsewhere.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the recipe-app arithmetic in C08.6.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chain: <strong>reach × baseline × lift = extra successes; × (1 − haircut); × value per success (causal share only) = impact.</strong> Then compare it to a second route and say the range.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c08-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C08-07 · The winners-and-losers table · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a proposed change and the segments it touches.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a table of who gains, who loses, how each is likely to react, and a mitigation, plus the measure that will show the harm if it happens.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can name the segments and anticipate their reactions.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> attention to losers can slow or dilute a good change; ignoring them can damage trust.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> naming no losers; a mitigation that is only communication; averaging results across segments so the harm disappears.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the table for the messaging change in Worked example 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Gains or loses</th>\n<th>Likely reaction</th>\n<th>Mitigation</th>\n<th>Measure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A change that wins on the average and hurts the most dependent segment is a decision to make knowingly, not by default.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c08-the-improvement-answer-for-a-45-minute-round\"></span>\n<h4>The improvement answer, for a 45-minute round</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarify the product and the goal</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Two or three questions; the role and goal stated as assumptions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Read the current state</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>The five-line state card: role, users, funnel, metrics, constraints</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Choose the area</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Segments, journey steps, a table; one chosen with a criterion; what you give up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Find the problem</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>One or two specific pain points at the step, with evidence or how you would get it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Generate moves</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Two to four moves of different types (fix, add, remove, reposition)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Size and choose</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>A sizing chain with a range; the recommendation with its stated cost; the first version and what comes next</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Primary metric, an input, a guardrail, a counter metric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risks</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Winners and losers; the constraint that matters; what would make you reverse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slack for interviewer questions</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the interviewer gives you the goal or the segment, move the saved time to the problem and the moves. If you run behind, drop to two moves, a one-sentence size and one risk, but keep the recommendation and the losers. Treat questions as steering and rejoin the spine.</p>\n<p><strong>What to clarify first.</strong> The product and the platform (the version the interviewer has in mind), any goal they want, and any constraint. Ask two or three and state assumptions for the rest. A useful opener: &quot;I'll assume we are improving the existing mobile experience for current users, and the goal is more people completing the core task. Tell me if you want something else.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Where to signpost.</strong> After clarifying: &quot;I'll cover what the product is for, where to focus, the problem, a few options, a size and a recommendation.&quot; Before choosing: &quot;I'll pick by reach and headroom.&quot; Before recommending: &quot;My deciding criterion is X, and I'll say who loses.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> Two sentences: &quot;I recommend [move] for [segment] at [step], to fix [problem], and I'd measure success by [metric]. It could hurt [segment], which I'd protect with [response], and I'd reverse if [condition].&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c08-type-the-goal-is-given\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the goal is given</h4>\n<p>&quot;Improve retention for X.&quot; Skip the goal choice, use the saved minutes on the segment and the step: where do the people who leave leave from? Use cohort thinking (C05) to say what you would look at.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-type-which-part-of-the-product-to-improve\"></span>\n<h4>Type: which part of the product to improve</h4>\n<p>&quot;What would you improve about X?&quot; Spend the first half on the selector (FW-C08-03), because the choice is the answer. Name two areas you considered and why you rejected the one you did.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-type-a-competitor-s-or-an-unfamiliar-product\"></span>\n<h4>Type: a competitor's or an unfamiliar product</h4>\n<p>Say that you know the product only from public information and use. State assumptions as such, label them, and build the answer on user needs and the funnel logic, not on claimed insider facts. Invite correction.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-type-the-short-version-20-to-25-minutes\"></span>\n<h4>Type: the short version (20 to 25 minutes)</h4>\n<p>Keep every step and halve the time: one segment, one step, two moves, a one-line size, one metric, one loser. Protect the recommendation and the losers.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What is this product for?&quot;</td>\n<td>You skipped the role</td>\n<td>State the role for company and users in a sentence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Why that part of the product?&quot;</td>\n<td>Your choice is unclear</td>\n<td>Give reach, pain and headroom, and what you rejected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How big could this be?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want sizing</td>\n<td>Give the chain with a range and the biggest haircut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What about the people who like it as it is?&quot;</td>\n<td>You skipped the legacy users</td>\n<td>Name them, the cost to them and a protection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Wouldn't this just move usage from somewhere else?&quot;</td>\n<td>Cannibalization</td>\n<td>Say where the usage comes from and count net</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Do you need to build anything?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want a fix or a removal</td>\n<td>Offer the cheaper move and compare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How would you know it worked?&quot;</td>\n<td>They want a metric</td>\n<td>Use the C05 template with a guardrail and counter metric</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c08-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All four examples are <code>Illustrative</code>. Where a real company's product type is the setting (examples 1 and 4), nothing here is a claim about that company's actual numbers, roadmap or decisions; every figure is an invented assumption. Each follows the spine in FW-C08-01 and is written as notes you could say aloud. A spoken answer is shorter.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-worked-example-1-consumer-improving-group-chats-in-a-large-m\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Consumer: improving group chats in a large messaging app (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a messaging product of the kind Meta operates. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;How would you improve group chats in a large messaging app?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Clarify and goal.</strong> I'd ask whether we are improving the existing mobile app for current users and what the company wants. Assume yes, and the goal is that group chats stay valuable to the people in them, so that people keep taking part. Goal ladder (FW-C07-02): the company wants groups to keep people on the product; the product goal is steady participation in groups; the user's goal is to stay in touch with the people in the group without drowning.</p>\n<p><strong>State card (FW-C08-02).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Line</th>\n<th>Assumption</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Role</td>\n<td>A core product and a retention anchor: people stay because their groups are there, which is a <a href=\"#gl-network-effect\">network effect</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Users</td>\n<td>Heavy group users (family, friends, neighborhood, workplaces); casual members of busy groups; group creators and admins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Funnel</td>\n<td>Create or join, first messages, steady participation, then mute or leave</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics</td>\n<td>Weekly active groups; messages per active member; mute and leave rates, which I'd treat as early warnings of decay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constraints</td>\n<td>A change for senders affects receivers; members in groups of 200 or more rely on current behavior; every device must behave the same</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Choosing the area (FW-C08-03).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment and step</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Pain or loss</th>\n<th>Headroom</th>\n<th>Fit with goal</th>\n<th>Choice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Members of large groups (50 or more), keeping up with a busy chat</td>\n<td>Medium: 20% of users are in one (invented)</td>\n<td>High: 45% mute within 30 days of joining (invented)</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Chosen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Group creators, inviting people</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Rejected: fewer people affected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Members of small groups, reading and replying</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low: 5% mute (invented)</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Rejected: little headroom</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> After a burst of messages, a member of a large group cannot find what matters to them (a mention, a reply to them, a plan with a time). They mute the group, which removes them from the conversation while they remain in it. The workarounds are evidence: leaving the group, or messaging a friend &quot;what did I miss?&quot; I'd confirm by interviewing members who muted, and by checking what share of mutes follow a day with more than 100 messages.</p>\n<p><strong>The four moves (FW-C08-05).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Move</th>\n<th>Idea</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fix</td>\n<td>Make mentions and replies to me always notify and be easy to see, since many members do not realize a reply was to them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add</td>\n<td>A catch-up view at the top of a chat: mentions of me, replies to me and plans with times, in order (rule-based, not a summary written by a model)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remove</td>\n<td>Limit the &quot;notify everyone&quot; action in groups over 100 to admins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reposition</td>\n<td>Treat large groups as communities with sub-topics</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sizing (FW-C08-06).</strong> Assume 4,000,000 people a month join a large group (invented). At a 45% mute rate within 30 days, 1,800,000 mute. If the catch-up view cuts muting by 10% in relative terms, the mute rate becomes 40.5%: 1,620,000 mute, which is 180,000 fewer. Haircut 40% for people who never see the view and novelty: 108,000. Members who do not mute are still active after eight weeks at 70%, against 25% of those who mute (invented), but that is a correlation, so credit 40% as causal: 108,000 × (0.70 − 0.25) × 0.4 = 19,440 more active members from each month's joiners, about 0.5% of 4,000,000. Second route: the mute rate falls 4.5 points, and an active-share difference of 45 points gives 4.5 × 45% ≈ 2 points of the joiners before haircuts; after haircuts that is about 0.5 points, which agrees. The gain is modest, so the first version should be cheap.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> &quot;I recommend the Fix and the Add together for the first version: reliable mentions and replies, and a rule-based catch-up view for large groups. It addresses the cause of muting by giving up the more ambitious reposition into communities. Next I'd add plan reminders, and I'd revisit the community idea if muting stays high among very large groups.&quot; The remove is held back because admins of big groups use the notify-everyone action, and I don't know why each does.</p>\n<p><strong>Winners and losers (FW-C08-07).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Gains or loses</th>\n<th>Likely reaction</th>\n<th>Mitigation</th>\n<th>Measure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Members of large groups</td>\n<td>Gain</td>\n<td>Use the view; mute less</td>\n<td>Show it only after a gap, not always</td>\n<td>Mute rate; eight-week active share</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Senders of general messages (not a mention or reply)</td>\n<td>May lose attention: their messages are not in the &quot;for me&quot; view</td>\n<td>Post less, or tag more</td>\n<td>Keep the full chronological view as the default and the catch-up view as an addition</td>\n<td>Messages per sender; replies per message</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Admins who use notify-everyone</td>\n<td>Unaffected in the recommendation</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>None</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Metrics.</strong> Primary: the share of new large-group members still active in the group after eight weeks. Input: the share who open the catch-up view at least once in two weeks. Guardrail: messages sent per active member in large groups, which must not fall. Counter metric: replies received per message for senders who did not mention anyone. Reverse if replies per message fall by more than 5% (invented).</p>\n<span id=\"c08-worked-example-2-enterprise-improving-reports-in-a-project-m\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Enterprise: improving reports in a project-management tool (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;Customers say the reports in our project-management tool are poor. How would you improve them?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Clarify and goal.</strong> Assume a tool sold per seat to companies, and the goal is renewals and expansion. The buyers are department heads, the daily users are team members, and the report readers are team leads and managers. I'd say that the buyer, the administrator and the daily user differ, and that reports mostly serve the first and the readers above them.</p>\n<p><strong>State card.</strong> <em>Role:</em> revenue engine, sold by seat. <em>Users:</em> team leads (build reports), executives (read them), team members (feed the data). <em>Funnel:</em> tasks are entered, then reviewed, then summarized into a report for a manager each week. <em>Metrics:</em> weekly report runs, share of accounts that use reports weekly, renewal rate. <em>Constraints:</em> customers have built weekly routines, scripts and dashboards on the current export and the programming interface.</p>\n<p><strong>Choosing the area.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment and step</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Pain or loss</th>\n<th>Headroom</th>\n<th>Fit</th>\n<th>Choice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Team leads assembling the weekly status report</td>\n<td>High: about half of accounts (invented)</td>\n<td>High: about two hours a week by hand (invented)</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High: reports drive renewals</td>\n<td>Chosen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Executives reading dashboards</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Rejected for now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Admins configuring permissions</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Rejected</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Leads export tasks to a spreadsheet and build the status report by hand each week, because the built-in reports are slow to load and show wrong totals when tasks are reassigned. I'd check the complaint categories in support tickets and watch a lead build one.</p>\n<p><strong>The four moves.</strong> <em>Fix:</em> correct the totals and the load time, which is cheap and earns trust. <em>Add:</em> a scheduled status summary that builds itself and arrives by email on a chosen day. <em>Remove:</em> retire the old custom report builder, used by 3% of accounts (invented), because it is hard to maintain; I would not do this first, since those accounts are likely the most dependent. <em>Reposition:</em> from a task tracker to a status-communication tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraint scan (FW-C08-04).</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Check</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Response</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Legacy users</td>\n<td>Who depends on today's export and report behavior?</td>\n<td>Accounts with scripts that read the export columns. Keep every existing column and name; add new fields instead of changing old ones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cannibalization</td>\n<td>Does it take usage from something else?</td>\n<td>It replaces manual spreadsheet work, not product usage, so it adds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ecosystem</td>\n<td>What else touches this?</td>\n<td>Integrations and the programming interface. Version the export, announce six months ahead, and track calls to the old format</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reversibility</td>\n<td>Can we undo it?</td>\n<td>The totals fix and the new summary are reversible behind a flag; a changed data format is not, so the format stays</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sizing.</strong> Assume 8,000 accounts (invented), 4,000 using reports weekly and 4,000 not. The scheduled summary moves 20% of non-users into weekly use: 800 accounts. Haircut 30% for accounts that would have started anyway: 560. Time saved: 560 accounts × 6 leads (invented) × 1 hour a week × 48 weeks ≈ 161,000 hours a year. Renewal: weekly users renew at 94% against 86% for others (invented), a correlation, so credit 30%: 560 × 0.08 × 0.3 ≈ 13 more renewed accounts, and at $20,000 a year each (invented), about $270,000. The time saved is large for customers and the renewal effect is modest, so I'd present the value as customer time and risk reduction, and not promise a revenue number.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> &quot;I recommend the Fix and the scheduled summary, keeping every existing export column unchanged, which gives leads their two hours back and removes the most common complaint, by giving up a bigger redesign of reporting. Next I'd add sharing to executives, and I'd retire the old builder only after finding out why those accounts use it.&quot; Metrics: primary, the share of accounts using a scheduled report weekly after 90 days; input, time from opening reports to sending the summary; guardrail, calls to the old export format (a rise would mean broken scripts), and support tickets about reports; counter metric, the share of scheduled summaries sent but not opened, which would show an automated output nobody reads. Reverse the summary if open rates fall below a threshold set in advance.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-worked-example-3-marketplace-increasing-repeat-bookings-in-a\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — Marketplace: increasing repeat bookings in a home-services marketplace (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;Improve a marketplace where customers book home services such as cleaning and repairs.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Clarify and goal.</strong> The goal given by the interviewer: more repeat bookings. I'd note it is a two-sided product, so a change for customers affects providers. State card: <em>Role:</em> a revenue engine that takes a commission. <em>Users:</em> first-time customers, returning customers, and providers of varying experience. <em>Funnel:</em> search, request, provider confirms, service, review, rebook. <em>Metrics:</em> the share of first-time customers who book again within 60 days (invented baseline 30%), commission, provider cancellation rate. <em>Constraints:</em> providers depend on the booking flow for income; there is a risk of customers and providers moving off the platform to rebook directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Choosing the area.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment and step</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Pain or loss</th>\n<th>Headroom</th>\n<th>Fit with repeat goal</th>\n<th>Choice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First-time customers, request to confirmed booking</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High: 35% of requests unconfirmed after 24 hours (invented)</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Medium: it serves the first booking, not the second</td>\n<td>Rejected for this goal; a separate effort</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Returning customers, rebooking after the first service</td>\n<td>Medium: about a third reach this stage (invented)</td>\n<td>Medium: they must search again and cannot see the same provider's availability</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Chosen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Providers, setting quotes</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Rejected</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>I name the rejection: the confirmation step has a larger loss, but it is not the step that decides a repeat booking, and a customer whose first request fails never gets to a second. If the goal had been total bookings, I would have chosen it.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> After a good first service, a customer who wants the same provider again has to search from the start, and cannot see when that person is free. Many will message the provider directly, which takes the booking off the platform. The workaround is the evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>The four moves.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Move</th>\n<th>Idea</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fix</td>\n<td>Put &quot;Book again&quot; on the completed booking, with the provider's next available times</td>\n<td>Cheap; touches one screen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add</td>\n<td>Recurring bookings (every two weeks, same provider)</td>\n<td>Larger; needs provider calendars</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remove</td>\n<td>Remove the post-service rating prompt that currently blocks the screen the customer sees right after the service</td>\n<td>Easy; ratings are valuable, so move it, don't delete it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reposition</td>\n<td>From &quot;find a pro&quot; to &quot;your home's regular helpers&quot;</td>\n<td>Large and slow</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Cannibalization and second-order effect.</strong> Easier rebooking reduces new-provider discovery: customers book their favorite and new providers get fewer first jobs. That is a <a href=\"#gl-second-order-effect\">second-order effect</a> on the supply side, so a counter metric should track it. It also reduces the incentive to leave the platform, which is a benefit that the headline metric will not show.</p>\n<p><strong>Sizing.</strong> Assume 200,000 first-time customers a month (invented). At 30% rebooking within 60 days, 60,000 rebook. The fix lifts it to 33%: 66,000, which is 6,000 more. Haircut 30% for customers who would have rebooked anyway: 4,200. At an average booking of $80 and a 20% commission (invented), 4,200 × $80 × 0.20 = $67,200 a month. Second route: the one-screen fix is cheap, and a three-point lift on a 30% base is plausible for a button moved from hidden to prominent; I'd accept the number as an order of magnitude and look at the cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> &quot;I recommend the Fix and the moved rating prompt in the first version, which gives returning customers a one-tap path to the same provider by giving up recurring bookings for now. If rebooking rises and customers ask for recurring service, I'd build the Add next. I'd switch to the larger move sooner if interviews show that most repeat customers want the same time every week.&quot; Metrics: primary, the share of first-time customers booking again within 60 days; input, taps on &quot;Book again&quot;; guardrails, provider cancellation rate and the average rating, which must not fall because the prompt moved; counter metrics, the share of first-time jobs going to providers with fewer than five reviews (new-provider starvation) and off-platform contact attempts.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-worked-example-4-photo-library-improving-how-people-find-the\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Photo library: improving how people find their photos (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a photo-library product of the kind Google operates. No fact about any real product is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The prompt.</strong> &quot;How would you improve a photo-storage and sharing product?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Clarify and goal.</strong> I'd assume the existing mobile and web product, and a goal of making the product the place people go when they want a photo. State card (FW-C08-02): <em>Role:</em> a utility and retention anchor for a wider set of services, with storage plans as a possible revenue link. <em>Users:</em> heavy collectors with years of photos and tens of thousands of items, casual users with a few hundred, and families sharing albums. <em>Funnel:</em> capture and back up, organize, find, share, revisit. The product is likely strong at the first step because it is automatic, and weakest at finding a specific photo years later. <em>Metrics:</em> backup volume (healthy), search use and search success (my hypothesis: weaker), shares. <em>Constraints:</em> years of stored data, a large base of habitual users, and privacy sensitivity of the content.</p>\n<p><strong>Choosing the area.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment and step</th>\n<th>Reach</th>\n<th>Pain or loss</th>\n<th>Headroom</th>\n<th>Fit</th>\n<th>Choice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy collectors, finding a specific photo</td>\n<td>Medium: 40% of monthly users search (invented)</td>\n<td>High: 45% of searches end without opening a result (invented)</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Chosen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Casual users, first backup</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Rejected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Families, sharing albums</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Rejected for now</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A person looking for &quot;that dinner in the spring&quot; scrolls by date, tries a few words, and gives up or asks a relative. Search on the content of photos and on everyday phrases is the weak point. Evidence I'd look for: searches with no opened result, repeated reformulations within a minute, and what people do next (scrolling, asking others).</p>\n<p><strong>The four moves.</strong> <em>Fix:</em> make search understand everyday words and dates, and show why a result matched. <em>Add:</em> automatic albums for events. <em>Remove:</em> remove the manual album clutter for casual users, which power users rely on, so I'd not do it without a check. <em>Reposition:</em> from storage to a &quot;memories&quot; product.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraint scan.</strong> Legacy users: people with carefully built manual albums, who must keep them. Ecosystem: the same library appears on phones, tablets and the web, so search must behave alike. Responsible design (FW-C07-06): automatic labeling can be wrong, and some people and moments should not resurface. A &quot;memories&quot; or &quot;on this day&quot; style feature can show a painful moment (a death, a breakup), and the design response is to let users hide specific people and dates, and to make unwanted resurfacing easy to turn off. Privacy: grouping photos by faces is sensitive, so it is opt-in and the user can see and delete the groupings.</p>\n<p><strong>Sizing.</strong> Assume 50,000,000 monthly users (invented). 40% search: 20,000,000. Search success rises from 55% to 60%: 1,000,000 more successful searchers a month. Haircut 30%: 700,000, which is 3.5% of searching users. I would stop the chain there, because the link from a successful search to a company outcome (storage plan upgrades, return visits) is weak and mostly speculation, and I'd say so. Present it as an experience gain and measure return visits afterward, and not promise revenue.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> &quot;I recommend the Fix for the first version: better search on everyday words and dates, with a short reason for each match. It addresses the largest observed loss by giving up the automatic albums and the repositioning, both larger. Next I'd add events, with the controls above. I'd switch to the Add if interviews show that people don't search but prefer to browse collections.&quot; Metrics: primary, search success (a result opened or shared within two minutes); input, the share of queries that return at least one result; guardrails, the rate of wrong-label complaints and the privacy-control usage; counter metric, the share of users who turn off suggestions, which would show that a feature meant to help is unwelcome.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;How would you improve an online grocery-delivery app?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c08-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd add more features: a recipe section, voice ordering, AR to see how the food looks, and loyalty rewards. I'd also make the app faster and prettier.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> a list of additions with no statement of what the product is for, who uses it, or where it falls short. Nothing is chosen, sized or measured, and no one is protected.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'll assume the goal is more weekly orders from people who already use the app. The app is a revenue engine and its core customers are households that order every week, so I'd start there. The funnel is browse, build a basket, check out, receive and review, then reorder. I'd expect the largest loss and effort at building the basket, because a weekly shopper buys many of the same items.</p>\n<p>&quot;Candidate areas: new shoppers at their first order, regular shoppers at rebuilding the basket, and everyone at delivery slots. I'd choose regular shoppers rebuilding the basket: they are the most frequent, the pain repeats every week, and there is headroom because they rebuild 20 items by hand. The problem is that it takes them about 12 minutes to rebuild the same basket (an assumption to verify).</p>\n<p>&quot;Moves: a fix to make 'order again' easier to find; an add, a prefilled basket of the usual items; a removal of promotional carousels that sit between the user and the basket; a reposition into a 'weekly staples' service. I'd recommend the prefilled basket with the usual items, starting from the last order. Metrics: orders per active household per month, with the time to build a basket as an input.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c08-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;Prefilling the basket has a cost that I'd name: browsing is where some of the unplanned items come from, so faster reordering could reduce basket size. That is cannibalization of discovery, so I'd put basket value per order as a counter metric and look at net order value, not only order frequency.</p>\n<p>&quot;Substitutions are a legacy-user issue. A prefilled basket assumes the items are available, and when something is out of stock, regular shoppers have strong preferences about what replaces it. A prefilled basket that surprises them damages trust, so the design needs to show availability before checkout and let them set substitution rules once.</p>\n<p>&quot;On sizing: if 60% of 1,000,000 weekly households (invented) rebuild a basket by hand and the prefill saves 8 of 12 minutes for half of them, that is real time saved, but the revenue effect depends on whether it raises orders per month, which I would test, not assume. Households that already order weekly have little room to order more, so the gain is mainly among those who order every few weeks and find the effort a barrier; I'd target that group in the test.</p>\n<p>&quot;The risk to others is the picking and delivery side: a prefilled basket concentrates demand on staple items and delivery slots at peak times, so I'd watch slot availability. I'd change my mind if interviews showed that the effort in the basket is deciding what to buy, not typing it, in which case the prefill would not help and the real lever is meal planning.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c08-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>States the product's role, users and funnel</td>\n<td>Names cannibalization and the legacy-user issue for the change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chooses a segment and a step by a criterion</td>\n<td>Sizes the gain, and says where the chain is weak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offers more than one type of move and recommends one</td>\n<td>Names the second-order effect on other sides of the product</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gives a metric with an input</td>\n<td>Adds a counter metric and the condition for reversing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c08-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Candidates commonly report improvement prompts about Google's own products, often at very large scale and across many countries <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Google has long published a stated philosophy that starts with focusing on the user <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Google, Ten things we know to be true]</code>. Practical consequence: say who the product serves at scale, include users on slower devices and connections, and note that a small percentage change affects a very large number of people. Appendix A has briefs on eight Google products to practice against.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon publishes Customer Obsession and Invent and Simplify as leadership principles <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles, as cited by several summaries]</code>. Candidates commonly report that improvement answers are expected to begin with the customer's problem and to show a bias for simplicity <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: offer a removal or a simplification as one of your moves, and say what the customer would notice.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Candidates commonly report that improvement prompts at Meta are paired with questions about tradeoffs between engagement and wellbeing or integrity, and with questions on how you would measure a change <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: pair each change with a counter metric, and consider what a change does to people on the other end of an interaction.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft's products often serve large enterprise customers with long-lived processes and integrations, and the company is widely described as valuing backward compatibility <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: separate the buyer, the administrator and the daily user, and show that you protect existing customers when you change behavior.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple designs hardware, software and services together and publishes platform guidelines and a privacy position <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple Human Interface Guidelines (developer.apple.com) and privacy pages; Apple's own description of its platforms and services]</code>. Third-party guides report that improvement prompts at Apple concern its own devices and weigh consistency across them and attention to detail <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: check each change across the user's other devices, say what stays on the device, and keep a change small if a larger one would make the experience inconsistent.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix publishes material on its culture and on its approach to experimentation <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo, June 2024; Netflix Tech Blog on experimentation]</code>. Candidates commonly report data-informed improvement questions <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: say what you would test, with which metric and how long, and be explicit about novelty effects that fade.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe documents a dated approach to versioning its programming interface so that existing integrations keep working when behavior changes <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Stripe API versioning documentation and engineering blog]</code>. Candidates report developer-facing improvement prompts <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: for products that other people build on, treat compatibility as a feature: version the change and give customers time.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c08-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Treating the product as new</td>\n<td>Your first sentence is a feature</td>\n<td>State the role, users and funnel first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Not knowing what the product is for</td>\n<td>You cannot say how it matters to the company</td>\n<td>Name the role and ask to be corrected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Improving what you personally dislike</td>\n<td>Your evidence is your own annoyance</td>\n<td>Segment, reach and headroom</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Choosing a step with no headroom</td>\n<td>Most people already succeed there</td>\n<td>Compare loss and what a good level would be</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Only adding features</td>\n<td>All your moves are new things</td>\n<td>Offer a fix and a removal beside the add</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Removing without asking why it exists</td>\n<td>You call something &quot;clutter&quot;</td>\n<td>Apply the fence test: who uses it and why was it built</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>No sizing</td>\n<td>You cannot say whether it is worth a quarter</td>\n<td>Give the chain, haircut it and say the range</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Treating correlation as the effect</td>\n<td>You credit all of the retention gap to the step</td>\n<td>Credit part of it, name the selection effect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>No losers</td>\n<td>Everyone gains in your answer</td>\n<td>Fill in the winners-and-losers table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Averaging over segments</td>\n<td>A good average hides harm to the core group</td>\n<td>Measure the most dependent segment separately</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c08-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-local-optimum\">local optimum</a> comes from C02 and <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> from C05.</p>\n<p><strong>Chesterton's fence</strong> (<code>MM-chestertons-fence</code>). Before removing something, learn why it was put there. A feature or rule that looks pointless may be protecting someone you cannot see: an enterprise account's script, a safety check, a legal requirement. In an interview, the fence test means asking &quot;who uses it and why was it built?&quot; before a removal, and it signals judgment that the cost of complexity is balanced against the cost of breaking something.</p>\n<p><strong>The local maximum</strong> (<code>MM-local-optimum</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-local-optimum\">local optimum</a>, C02). Repeated small improvements to a step can raise a metric to the best a design can reach without making the product better for the person. A checkout flow can be tuned until little more can be gained while the real problem is that people do not want what they are checking out. When the improvements you can think of have small sizes, say so, and consider a larger move such as a reposition.</p>\n<p><strong>Loss aversion</strong> (<code>MM-loss-aversion</code>). People feel losing something familiar more strongly than gaining something of equal value, which is why a change that is better on average can feel worse to the people who lose a habit. It is the reason legacy users react strongly and the reason for a gradual path: let them keep the old way for a time, and show what they gain before you take away what they have.</p>\n<p><strong>Goodhart's law applied</strong> (<code>MM-goodhart</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, C05). When a measure becomes a target, it stops being a good measure. An improvement that raises a step's completion by making it easier to tap through, or that pushes notifications until sessions rise, can raise the number and lower the value to the user. Name the counter metric whenever you name the target.</p>\n<span id=\"c08-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> An existing product carries history: older code, data formats and integrations that make a change cheaper or costlier than it looks. Ask early what a change touches, what cannot be changed without a migration, and how the change can be released gradually (C17). Listen when an engineer says &quot;that field is used by something else.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> A designer knows the existing patterns that users have learned and the consistency the product depends on. Ask how much of the change users must relearn, and watch current users attempt the old and the new flow; a change that tests well with new users can still break a habit in core users.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Existing products have data, and the question is what it can and cannot show. Ask for the funnel and the segment breakdown before choosing, agree what counts as success and the counter metric, and check whether a difference between users of a feature and non-users comes from the feature or from who chose it (C14 on experiments).</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c08-checklist-before-you-recommend-a-change-to-an-existing-produ\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you recommend a change to an existing product</h3>\n";
